################################################################################
# The Windows QEMU test tiers (qemu_test) start one VM each. How many run at once:
# - Each test claims its VM's 4 vCPUs and 4700 MB (resources:cpu/memory tags), so
#   Bazel runs as many VMs as the host's CPUs and memory hold: 2 on 8 cores.
# - In CI, --config=ci's CPU load scheduling ignores the CPU claim and the pod's
#   memory limit (which HOST_RAM follows) sets the number of VMs:
#   HOST_RAM*.67 / 4700 MB. Size a CI pod for the VMs it should run, with 4 CPUs
#   each.
# - Each test also claims one qemu_vm (resources:qemu_vm:1), a limit of its own
#   for running fewer VMs than the CPUs and memory allow. Bazel fails a test whose
#   tag names an undeclared resource, so qemu_vm is declared below as HOST_CPUS,
#   which limits nothing: the CPU or memory claim binds first. To run at most N
#   VMs at once, e.g. 1 to keep a laptop responsive, pass
#   --local_resources=qemu_vm=N or put it in user.bazelrc. Whichever of the
#   three limits is lowest wins.
# - Bazel ignores all test resource tags under a non-zero --local_test_jobs:
#   never pass it with these tiers (they would run as many VMs as test jobs).
# None of this is the licence limit: VMs on a developer's machine run under their
# own MSDN licence, and in CI a cluster quota caps the Windows VMs of all builds
# together (CMK-41343).
# --config=qemu includes --config=test-vm, without which the tiers are incompatible.
# Example: bazel test --config=qemu //agents/wnx:watest-qemu
################################################################################
common --local_resources=qemu_vm=HOST_CPUS
common:qemu --config=test-vm
